A fatwa is essentially a legal opinion or ruling issued by an Islamic scholar, known as a mufti, under Islamic law (Sharia). It is a response to a question about how to apply Islamic principles to a specific situation or issue. Fatwas can cover a wide range of topics, from worship and rituals to social and political matters. They are based on the interpretation of Islamic texts, including the Quran and Hadith (the sayings and actions of the Prophet Muhammad).

One of the critical aspects of a fatwa is that it is non-binding. This means that while a fatwa can provide guidance on what Islamic law suggests about a particular issue, it does not have the force of law unless it is adopted by a relevant legal authority. Furthermore, different Islamic scholars can issue different fatwas on the same issue, reflecting the diversity of opinion within Islamic jurisprudence.

